I'd say don't even get burdened at all.  Just leave the stuff you
don't immediately need in a pile somewhere and make a note of it.  You
can come back and pick it up later.

Speed is very important in nethack, especially if your character is
not a juggernaut that can melee its way through everything.  Potions
and wands of speed often self-identify because monsters use them on
themselves; when you get some yourself, don't hesitate to use them.
Speed boots are better than either -- try on non-cursed footwear in
case you should find some.  Jumping boots are useful too.

> one halfway between the two. Use your pet to check which armor you can
> use and wear the stuff you *know* not to be cursed so you can get your
> AC down.

Good advice.  Also, don't settle for the first armor you find -- try
on anything your pet will step on in case it turns out to be better.
Yes, testing all those countless orcish helms does get tedious, but
finding the blessed +3 one is reward enough.  Even for a wizard -- in
the early game the extra AC is more important, and you can always take
it off for spellcasting.

Elven armor is always worth trying: it has good base AC, good MC and,
except for mithril, won't interfere with spellcasting.  The stealth
effect is nothing to sneer at either.

(Elves are good eating too, if you're not one yourself.  You should
always try to eat anything that might give useful intrinsics.  Poison
resistance is one of the most important ones -- there are only a few
monsters that can give it without being poisonous themselves, though
once you have a blessed unicorn horn you can try the poisonous ones
too -- but the sooner you get them all the better.)

> I do this the first time after returning from the mines, but

I see no reason to wait until then.  The first few levels are when the
AC (and MC) from random armor is most important.  Once you've got more
hit points, better natural AC and the means to enchant your armor, you
can afford to be more picky.  Until then, try on everything you find
that isn't cursed, and keep whatever gives you the most AC.

